module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.json_pointer_dot_spelling_reads_nested_fields_as_absent

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data json_pointer_dot_spelling_reads_nested_fields_as_absent: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "json_pointer_dot_spelling_reads_nested_fields_as_absent" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a JSON pointer spelled with dots where the transport expects slashes yields a successful exchange whose nested fields read as absent** (an operation declares output paths such as `result.id`; the response arrives well-formed with those members nested; the path resolver treats the dotted spelling as one key, not a descent; RestOk fires; every nested projection is NULL.",

    "INVALID STATE: a decided success rendered as a read failure. The HTTP exchange completed. The body was well-formed. The model saw nothing. Unlike an undecided 5xx or timeout, the outcome channel says RestOk, so a consumer that treats only those statuses as commit-ambiguous will treat the mint as unread rather than as already committed.",

    "HARM WHEN THE OPERATION IS NOT IDEMPOTENT: performing the external mutation again. The specimen minted two live credentials on a real account while the local record of them was none -- the same harm the commit-ambiguous rule exists to prevent, reached by a different route.",

    "SPECIMEN, measured live (2026-09-10, gunbc#10947). `extdeps.cloudflare.account_api_tokens` Create projected `id`, `value`, `status`, `name` from `result.id` / `result.value` / `result.status` / `result.name`. Two Creates returned RestOk. Nested fields were unread. Tokens `3cbb99f150c4daec03ab6a8458a2440f` and `cb444492f55dbc1b40ee7ce17778d7ff` (both named `gunbai-fabric-origin-object-read`) were listed on the account as active, then revoked by that name. A third Create, after the paths became `result/id` and siblings, is the stored mint (`3f43e1c7f8fbcfc97139cb5d9dc4e984`). The account listing was the readback; the revoked identities come from that listing, not from the unread Create projections.",

    "WHAT SAVED IT IS THE AMBIGUITY DISCIPLINE APPLIED TO A CASE IT WAS NOT WRITTEN FOR. List the account tokens; revoke the unheld name; do not retry into an unread RestOk as if the first mint had not landed. The rule named 5xx and timeouts; RestOk is a decided outcome. The listing still prevented a third untracked credential.",

    "SCOPE IS THE PATH EXPRESSION, NOT THIS VENDOR AND NOT THIS ENDPOINT. Any output-path spelling that can silently miss on a well-formed response, where the operation is not idempotent, is the class. Dots versus slashes is the specimen grammar; a wrong separator, an extra segment, or a flattened key that the envelope never uses as a leaf will do the same.",

    "BOUNDED AGAINST `wire_projection_names_a_field_the_envelope_lacks`. That class names a field the envelope never carries (`from \"body\"` on `{ result, success, errors, messages }`). This class names a location the envelope DOES carry, under a pointer grammar the resolver does not apply. Both render as RestOk plus NULL. The recognition rule here is: the nested members exist; the spelling cannot reach them.",

    "RUNG: found BELOW the ladder on the first two Creates -- silent wrongness about whether a non-idempotent mutation had effects -- then mitigated by an independent List. The RestOk arm itself still cannot see the miss.",

    "CEILING 4, structurally impossible: a path expression validated against the declared response shape, so a pointer that cannot match refuses at DECLARATION rather than at read. A runtime refusal when a required path is Null is the affordable intermediate (rung 1) and must not be mistaken for that trigger.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: any output-path spelling that can silently miss on a well-formed response, where the operation is not idempotent. Review tell: dotted nested paths beside slash-path siblings on the same transport, or a RestOk whose required nested fields print as null while the upstream listing shows the mutation landed.)",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
